<h2>The investigation</h2>
<p>Acting on secret information, the cyber police found that a bank account held by co-accused Karan was operating as a mule account for the proceeds of online frauds committed across the country. Karan&rsquo;s disclosure statement implicated the applicant: he said he had handed over his banking credentials, withdrawn the fraud money, and passed the cash on after keeping a commission of 2%. Beyond that disclosure, the prosecution relied on WhatsApp chats between Karan and the applicant — chats which, notably, did not find place in the status report filed before the court.</p>

<h2>The chats, read aloud</h2>
<p>Rather than accept a characterisation of the chats, the court had the Investigating Officer read them out from his record at the hearing. The entirety of the chat evidence proved to be two messages texted by the applicant — one asking about a small sum of Rs 200, the other asking for a withdrawal by a particular time. Confronted with this, the prosecution admitted that &ldquo;there is no other evidence against the present accused/applicant&rdquo;. The court also noted that co-accused Karan and Raja had already been granted bail.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;I find no reason to deprive the accused/applicant liberty.&rdquo; The application was allowed, with a direction that the applicant be released on bail in the event of arrest.</p>
</div>

<h2>The legal footing</h2>
<p>Three strands converge in the order. First, a disclosure statement of a co-accused carries limited evidentiary value — it is not, by itself, the kind of material that justifies pre-trial custody. Second, the content of electronic communications matters more than their existence: two facially innocuous messages do not become incriminating because they occur between an accused and an account holder. Third, parity: with the account holder himself on bail, custodial interrogation of the alleged cash handler stood on still weaker ground. The order is compact — ten paragraphs — but its method is instructive: the court tested the prosecution&rsquo;s file in open court instead of deferring to its summary.</p>

<h2>Conditions imposed</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Personal bond of Rs 20,000 with one surety of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the IO/SHO concerned.</li>
  <li>The applicant shall join the investigation as and when directed in writing by the Investigating Officer.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Mule-account cases: the pattern in Delhi</h2>
<p>Cyber police stations across Delhi register a steady stream of FIRs tracing defrauded money into layered bank accounts. The account holder is usually the first arrest; disclosure statements then radiate outward to friends, relatives and acquaintances said to have handled cash or provided credentials. This judgment illustrates the judicial checkpoint in that process: at the anticipatory bail stage, the court asks what admissible material connects this applicant to the offence — not what the investigative narrative asserts. Where the answer is a co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure plus ambiguous chats, pre-arrest protection follows, subject to the standing obligation to join the investigation.</p>
<p>For those on the other side of such cases — complainants who have lost money to online frauds — the decision changes little about the recovery process: freezing of accounts, refund applications before the designated authorities, and prosecution of the principal operators proceed on their own track. The bail stage filters custody, not liability.</p>
